# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NextMovie — local environment template
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in real values:
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and must NEVER be committed.
# This file documents which variables exist; it must contain no real secrets.
#
# SCOPE: this file is read by **docker-compose only**.
#
#   - The API does not read .env at all — .NET has no such convention. Its local
#     settings live in appsettings.Development.json, and its secrets in .NET
#     user-secrets. See "API configuration" below.
#   - The web app reads apps/web/.env.local, not this file: Next.js loads env
#     files from its own project directory. See apps/web/.env.example.
#
# Putting a value here and expecting either app to see it is the mistake this
# comment exists to prevent.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- PostgreSQL (consumed by docker-compose.yml) ----------------------------
# POSTGRES_PORT is the HOST port. It is 5433 rather than the default 5432 to
# avoid colliding with a Homebrew PostgreSQL service on this machine. The
# container itself still listens on 5432 inside the compose network.
POSTGRES_USER=nextmovie
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=localdev_change_me
POSTGRES_DB=nextmovie
POSTGRES_PORT=5433

# The API's own connection string is NOT here — it is in
# appsettings.Development.json, which is what the API actually reads. These
# POSTGRES_* values and that connection string describe the same database, so
# changing one means changing the other.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API configuration
#
#   appsettings.Development.json   ConnectionStrings:NextMovieDb, Google:ClientIds
#                                  (committed; no secrets)
#   .NET user-secrets              Tmdb:ApiReadAccessToken, Jwt:SigningKey
#                                  (never in the repo)
#
# Google:ClientIds is the allow-list of OAuth client IDs whose ID tokens the API
# will accept (ADR-0005). Client IDs are public, but the list is a security
# boundary: a token minted for someone else's client must never sign anybody in
# here. The API refuses to start with an empty list; the committed placeholder
# matches no real token, which is exactly what you want until you add one.
#
# In deployed environments both come from environment variables instead
# (ConnectionStrings__NextMovieDb, Google__ClientIds__0), since .NET does map
# double underscores onto configuration keys — it just does not read .env files.
#
# Web configuration lives in apps/web/.env.local — see apps/web/.env.example.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API secrets are NOT stored here.
#
# The API reads them via .NET user-secrets, which keeps them outside the repo
# directory entirely (~/.microsoft/usersecrets) and removes any chance of a
# stray `git add .` committing them:
#
#     cd apps/api/NextMovie.Api
#     dotnet user-secrets set "Tmdb:ApiReadAccessToken" "<your-v4-token>"
#     dotnet user-secrets set "Jwt:SigningKey" "$(openssl rand -base64 48)"
#
# Get a TMDb token at https://www.themoviedb.org/settings/api (free).
# Prefer the v4 "API Read Access Token" (a bearer JWT) over the v3 api_key.
#
# Jwt:SigningKey signs access tokens (HS256), so it must be at least 32
# characters and is as sensitive as the database password: anyone holding it can
# mint a token for any account. Use a different key per environment — a leaked
# development key must never be able to sign production tokens. The API refuses
# to start without one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
